WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:12.700
From motion capture to virtual production,

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.720
Disney has been able to bring everything

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.610
from the Pride Lands to
Agrabah into the real world.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:20.740
But trying to translate these classics

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:23.400
from hand-drawn animation to live action

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.210
while maintaining
everything audiences loved

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:28.780
about the originals is never easy.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:31.530
Like, how do you make
a photorealistic lion

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.339
that can also belt out songs?

00:00:34.339 --> 00:00:36.930
♪ It means no worries ♪

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:39.880
♪ For the rest of your days ♪

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:41.150
To see how they did it,

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:44.260
take a peek at what eight
Disney live-action remakes

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:46.505
looked like behind the scenes.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:53.310
Actress Liu Yifei
trained for three months,

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.660
about six to seven hours per day,

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:56.980
to portray Mulan.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.060
Like here, where you can see her preparing

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.550
for one of the movie's epic sword fights.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:03.890
According to cinematographer Mandy Walker,

00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:07.590
the actress did about
90% of her own stunts.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:09.460
The cast had to learn everything

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.650
from martial arts to horseback riding

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.360
for the action-packed film.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.720
Meanwhile, many of the
film's gravity-defying stunts

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.223
were achieved with impressive wire work.

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:26.000
This film featured about
70 different animal species

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.170
created using groundbreaking CGI.

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:31.410
There was a key step in the process

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:34.670
to making sure all of the
animals looked so realistic:

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:35.750
facial rigging.

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:38.170
During this stage, animators explored

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:41.410
what muscles animals
have that humans do not.

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:43.720
By doing that, they
could ensure the animals

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:46.640
would do things like snarl the right way.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:47.948
To make sure the animals

00:01:47.948 --> 00:01:49.767
looked convincing when they talked,

00:01:49.767 --> 00:01:51.467
animators used a combination

00:01:51.467 --> 00:01:53.333
of human and animal muscles

00:01:53.333 --> 00:01:54.930
for the characters' mouths.

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:56.950
Another crucial breakthrough had to do

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.960
with putting hairs on the animals.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:00.520
They had to figure out the direction

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:03.030
an animal's hair would
move, how it clumps,

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:05.940
and how it interacts
with the world around it.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:07.820
Perhaps most impressive is that

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:09.760
Neel Sethi, who played Mowgli,

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.403
acted completely alone on set,

00:02:12.403 --> 00:02:15.650
as all the animals were created with CGI

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:18.510
and the other stars recorded their voices.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.800
But he did have a little company.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.333
Actors in blue suits and crew members

00:02:23.333 --> 00:02:25.158
held up animal puppets

00:02:25.158 --> 00:02:27.380
and used googly eyes on their hands,

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:30.680
like these pesky monkeys
that steal food from Mowgli,

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:34.233
which were later replaced with
CGI versions of the animals.

00:02:38.737 --> 00:02:43.222
"The Jungle Book" paved the
way for 2019's "The Lion King."

00:02:43.222 --> 00:02:45.973
The film was shot using
virtual production,

00:02:45.973 --> 00:02:48.760
which allowed director
Jon Favreau and the crew

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.460
to step into the world they were creating

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.960
and shoot everything like
it was really live action.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.980
The biggest challenge for the
film's visual effects team

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:00.210
was creating a photorealistic version

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:02.150
of the African savanna while also

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:04.700
remaining faithful to the source material.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.920
Take the part where Rafiki
stands and lifts Simba.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.170
In reality, mandrills
can't stand up like that.

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:12.460
So in the new version,

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:14.770
they animated the character sitting down

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:17.150
while lifting Simba into the air.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.960
And the animals still
needed to be able to speak

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:23.020
and also sing some of those iconic songs.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.310
So the animators would try to reposition

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:27.080
the animals' heads when they could

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:29.110
so the audience wouldn't be staring

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:30.820
directly into their mouths.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:33.670
And they timed the characters'
breathing to their dialogue

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:35.860
and at the same time let the characters'

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:38.240
belly muscles and diaphragm tighten

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:39.950
so it would really like they were

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:42.141
letting out air while talking.

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:50.167
Many CGI dogs were created
for this live-action remake.

00:03:50.167 --> 00:03:52.940
However, the two main stars were real.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:55.010
A mutt named Monte played Tramp,

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:58.310
while a cocker spaniel
named Rose played Lady.

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:00.580
Perhaps nothing was more challenging

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:02.563
than filming the most iconic scene

00:04:02.563 --> 00:04:04.690
from the 1955 original,

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:06.398
when the two dogs share a plate

00:04:06.398 --> 00:04:08.120
of spaghetti and meatballs.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.590
This scene took three days to film,

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:13.240
and while shooting it,
the noodles kept breaking.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:15.710
So instead of using actual noodles,

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:19.310
they used undyed licorice
soaked in chicken broth.

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:22.450
This ensured that the noodle
was strong enough for the take

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:26.108
and also that the dogs had
something quite tasty to eat.

00:04:26.108 --> 00:04:28.770
The moment where the two
dogs' snouts touch, though?

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:30.439
That was CGI.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:36.120
The elephants were created with masterful

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:39.305
visual effects work by MPC Film.

00:04:39.305 --> 00:04:41.460
However, the actors still needed

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.240
something on set to sell the scenes.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.190
So an actor and a stunt
double in green suits

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:49.120
stood in for baby Dumbo.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.680
But first, they had to learn
to move like a baby elephant,

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:54.200
which included walking around

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:56.710
on all fours using these stilts,

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:59.423
no easy feat on long shoot days.

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:04.510
Instead of making the Genie character

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:06.870
an entirely CGI creation,

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:10.810
Will Smith got to fully embody
him and his dance moves,

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:14.270
and it was all captured
by a motion-capture suit.

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:16.900
While "Aladdin" initially
received criticism

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:19.300
for how the Genie looked in his blue form,

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:20.680
the movie ended up having him

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:22.960
in human form whenever it could.

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:26.070
And to get a sense of some of
the film's impressive stunts,

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.728
just look at the "One
Jump Ahead" sequence.

00:05:28.728 --> 00:05:32.130
While most movies are shot
at 24 frames per second,

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:33.784
some parts of this sequences were shot

00:05:33.784 --> 00:05:36.500
at a slow-motion 36 frames per second,

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:39.080
while others were shot
at an incredibly fast

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:41.330
18 frames per second.

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:43.210
According to director Guy Ritchie,

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:45.490
actor Mena Massoud had to make sure

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:47.770
he was singing completely in sync.

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:50.550
So at some parts he
had to sing really fast

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:52.713
and other parts really slow.

00:05:56.507 --> 00:05:58.270
"Mistress of Evil" is a sequel

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:01.320
to the 2014 retelling of "Sleeping Beauty"

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:03.030
from the villain's perspective.

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:05.760
This movie had to make
its titular character,

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.200
and many other characters, fly.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:11.340
They pulled this off using
two different methods.

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:13.190
The first was using wires,

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:15.230
a more traditional practical method

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:17.260
for lifting up characters in the air.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:20.350
The second way was lifting
actress Angelina Jolie

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:22.280
and the other flying characters up

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:24.410
with these two-pronged forks.

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:26.790
The forks are operated by these extras

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:29.090
in blue suits that you see back here,

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:32.400
who would later be turned
into CGI backdrops.

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:35.690
The fork fits onto both
sides of a person's body.

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:37.630
This device gives them mobility

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:39.410
and allows them to twist a person around

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:42.212
backwards, forwards, or side to side,

00:06:42.212 --> 00:06:44.130
a "360-degree movement"

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:47.080
while the actor is safely
clamped into place.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:50.800
Meanwhile, Maleficent's
wings were created with CGI.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:53.460
While on set, Jolie
wore these on her back,

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:55.690
which would later be replaced with wings.

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:57.330
The visual effects team was tasked

00:06:57.330 --> 00:06:58.910
with making convincing wings

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:01.950
and also making sure
those wings moved in sync

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:03.923
with the performer's body movements.

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:09.690
To fully embody the role of Beast,

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:13.830
actor Dan Stevens wore a
motion-capture suit on stilts.

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:15.703
The stilts ensured that the actor

00:07:15.703 --> 00:07:18.080
was the Beast's appropriate height on set

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:20.640
so the actors would be
looking at the right spot.

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:22.830
However, they didn't
just have the characters

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:24.520
standing in front of green screens;

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:27.750
they actually built many
of these sets in England,

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:29.400
and they are impressive.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:31.030
Take the ballroom, which was made

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:34.240
with 12,000 square feet of faux marble.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:37.210
And they payed special
attention to the costumes.

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:39.050
Belle's dress was decorated

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:43.290
with about 2,160 Swarovski crystals,

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:45.490
and according to actress Emma Watson,

00:07:45.490 --> 00:07:47.530
the costume designers created it

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:51.820
so it looked like it was floating
as Belle and Beast danced.

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:54.540
What are some of your favorite
Disney live-action remakes?

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:56.990
And which ones are you
most looking forward to?

00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:58.981
Let us know in the comments.

